# Axis: enforcement claimed vs existing
COUNT: 25

## Findings
1. **Structure beats Willpower** — claims the registry “self-applies through the `scripts/standards-coverage.mjs` ratchet,” which fails CI on named-guard coverage — this detects whether articles cite guards and preserve a coverage ratio, not whether those guards detect the articles’ behavioral breaches. The surface is repeatedly corroborated, but it can classify paperwork as enforcement.
2. **Operator-Surface Quality** — claims `scripts/instar-dev-precommit.js` “blocks a commit touching an operator surface unless the review answers the quality question in writing” — this detects a written declaration, not whether the surface is usable, plain-language, mobile-safe, or visually sound. The surface is plausible and shared by other articles, but the article explicitly uses this receipt to classify itself as enforced.
3. **Side-Effects Review Gate** — says “The review is a structural gate” enforced by a staged side-effects artifact — the gate certifies only artifact presence and minimum length, not review of the five required dimensions, reviewer independence, or a substantive verdict. The article eventually admits this is “willpower with a receipt,” but only after the strong enforcement claim.
4. **Dashboard UX Standard** — claims “eleven objective, CI-enforced floors” — its enforcement section names tests only for F1, F2, F9, F10, and F11; F3–F8 are explicitly follow-up work. Those tests plausibly detect real covered UI breaches, but six of the eleven advertised floors do not exist yet; the correction is buried at the end of a long Applied-through paragraph.
5. **Agent Proposes, Operator Approves** — claims a precommit assertion “blocks a commit … unless answered in writing,” plus one server-authored reference implementation — the generic gate detects an attestation, not whether every approval surface is pre-filled or display-integrity-safe. The named infrastructure is plausible, but only the first implementation has behavioral structure.
6. **Self-Hosting** — says the dogfood-to-ship test is enforced by a Standards-Conformance Gate that is “live and AUTO-INVOKED” — at most that gate can semantically flag a plan, and the article says it is signal-only. More seriously, the registry’s joining rules later say the same registry-wide conformance gate “is not yet live” and “the registry does not yet enforce itself.” The surface is cross-referenced, but its live status is internally contradictory.
7. **Conservative Outbound: Act, Don’t Notify** — claims an enforcement package including “a lint that flags a new proactive … emitter” — the same article first says “nothing checks a new emitter’s default at build time” and labels the work tracked as CMT-1901. Existing topic-routing and flood guards detect volume/destination breaches, not whether the operator genuinely needed a notification. The claimed disposition lint is therefore dangling or future work.
8. **Constitutional Traceability — No Unconstitutional Work** — claims a “blocking, auto-invoked ship-gate” — the deterministic arm only checks that `parent-principle` names a real article, a well-formed declaration that may be substantively false. The semantic arm could detect weak fit, but fails open when unavailable and leaves no record of that bypass. The surface is plausible, yet the absolute “no work ships” claim is stronger than it.
9. **No Deferrals** — says the orphan-deferral precommit step enforces the rule by requiring a marker/frontmatter field — this detects declaration of tracking, not ownership, deadline, active follow-through, closure, or even all ordinary-language deferrals. The article admits the lifecycle is unenforced, but only after initially presenting the marker gate as enforcement.
10. **Canonical Pipeline Operational Completeness — Accepted Intake Must Drain** — claims a manifest, lint, and named runtime positive control enforce operational completeness — the lint detects declared stages, citations, metadata, and collected test entries; it explicitly cannot certify actual liveness, cadence, idempotency, or progress, and an undeclared intake can escape the closed manifest. Plausible machinery, but mostly structural declaration rather than proof that intake drains.
11. **Migration-Consumer Completeness** — claims marker/revision lint plus tests enforce complete migration of every consumer — this detects synchronized declarations among enrolled files, not an omitted consumer that was never enrolled. The article concedes semantic completeness to review; the named lint is plausible but cannot detect the crystallizing breach class without a complete declaration.
12. **An Instar Agent Is Always a Multi-Machine Entity** — claims a deterministic marker floor and strengthened review — the lint checks presence and taxonomy-valid form of `machine-local-justification`, not whether machine-locality is justified or whether the feature is actually unified. It is also report-first/non-blocking. The behavioral decision remains with review, so the mechanical surface is paperwork enforcement.
13. **Self-Heal Before Notify** — claims `scripts/lint-self-heal-fields.js` as its structural floor — it checks that a self-heal declaration contains fields and a non-empty remediation list, not that remediation works or that notification is downstream of exhausted healing. It is report-first and non-blocking; substantive judgment remains a reviewer question.
14. **Iterative Audit to Convergence** — claims the `converged:` stamp is “machine-earned” — the machinery can detect malformed or insufficiently populated audit records, but not a shallow audit, an unchanged question, or the more likely breach where no audit record is opened. The guard is plausible and real for form, not for thoroughness or initiation.
15. **One Failure Teaches Every Guard** — claims gap-record and fingerprint lints make each failure sweep every guard — they enforce freshness, partitioning, and reasons over voluntarily authored records. They cannot detect an unrecorded failure, judge sweep quality, or require a match to be remediated. The article accurately calls this “freshness bookkeeping,” but only deep inside extensive enforcement prose.
16. **Maturation Path — Test Agent → Development Agent → Fleet** — claims `FeatureMaturationPlanGate` refuses convergence without a complete plan — that detects the declaration required by clause (a), not graduation-evidence quality, ship-time registration, routing, or whether the ladder is ever walked. Three of four clauses remain unenforced; the named gate is plausible but certifies paperwork.
17. **Observation Needs Structure** — claims several cycle, verdict, trace, and persistence gates as its application — those can detect missing artifacts for their specific duties, but nothing enumerates all observation duties or requires each to have such an artifact. The universal rule is supported by examples, not a universal guard; the gap appears inside In-practice rather than as a documented-only label.
18. **Mobile-Complete Operator Actions** — claims one Mandates UI, migrated guidance, and an operator-surface review question — the first implementation may detect its own behavior, while the generic mechanism only asks for a declaration and does not enumerate operator-required actions. The article’s known missing approval-link generalization is visible, but it is not labeled documented-only despite the universal “every action” rule.
19. **Live-User-Channel Proof Before Done** — claims `LiveTestGate` refuses done/shipped without a verified artifact — this can detect a real missing artifact for a feature declared user-facing and configured in veto mode. It cannot detect an undeclared user-facing feature, and dry-run/warn modes do not block. The limitation is stated in Applied-through, but the article title and completion-gate language invite broader trust.
20. **A Decision That Can Block Must Live Where the Checks Can See It** — claims `lint-blocking-decisions-declared.mjs` covers the blocking population — it detects declarations, vanished files, ceiling growth, and changed bytes in files declared non-blocking. A changed file already declared blocking is silent, and completeness depends on the seeded census. It guards population bookkeeping, not continued correctness of blocking decisions.
21. **The User Experience Is the Product** — presents seven structural substandards, but Applied-through says only substandard #4 has its “first structural teeth”; #1, #2, #3, #5, #6, and #7 are tracked fixes. It also calls the article an “operator-ratifiable proposal” only at the end, rather than marking pending status under the heading. One real tone-gate behavior cannot enforce the seven-part invariant.
22. **No Unbounded Loops** — claims application through several existing breakers and a restart-survival test — these can detect breaches in named components, not “any code path that repeats.” The universal audit is “IN PROGRESS, not landed,” making the rule an instruction today. That admission is present but buried at the end of Applied-through.
23. **Deferral = Deletion** — claims a “FUNCTION check rather than an existence check” through `lint-deferral-referent-resolves.mjs` — the function actually tests whether an identifier occurs in selected executable/structured repository content. It cannot detect capture-now breaches, distinguish abandoned from completed work, or verify terminal follow-through. The article eventually documents this honestly, but only after an unusually strong claim and extensive corrective history.
24. **The Body and the Mind** — claims application through tier classifiers, LLM-supervised gates, and audit trails — these are examples, not a population-wide guard on consequential authority decisions. Its “Documented-only until” label comes after Applied-through at the article’s very end, so a scanning reader sees apparent enforcement before the actual status.
25. **A Metric Must Measure the Work, Not the Question** — its fingerprint correctly says “moments: none; surfaces: nothing,” but the “Documented-only until” label is buried near the end of a very long Traces-to-the-goal paragraph rather than surfaced under the heading. By contrast, **Close the Loop** and **Session Input Is a Principal** label documented-only status conspicuously, and **Observable Intelligence** marks pending status immediately below its heading.

## Articles reviewed
89